<Project>

  <!--
    Shared packaging hardening for the NuGet packages this repository ships.

    These properties only take effect during `pack`, so they are inert for every other project that
    also imports this file (the tests, the samples, any tooling).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- SourceLink lets debuggers step into the exact commit's sources from the published symbols.
         Since .NET 8 the SDK bundles Source Link and auto-detects the GitHub remote, so no
         Microsoft.SourceLink.* PackageReference is needed; setting these properties is enough. -->
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>

    <!-- Ship symbols as a separate .snupkg so the main package stays lean but stays debuggable. -->
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>

    <!-- Deterministic, path-normalized builds on CI; a no-op for local builds. -->
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Warning ratchet: the codebase builds with ZERO warnings, and CI locks that state in by turning every
    warning into an error — a new warning can never merge, so the count can never creep up. Scoped to CI
    (same GITHUB_ACTIONS signal as ContinuousIntegrationBuild above) so local builds stay friendly to
    iteration: half-finished refactorings may warn without blocking the inner loop.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">
    <!-- Two switches are needed for a TOTAL ratchet. TreatWarningsAsErrors is compiler-scoped (it only
         promotes CS* diagnostics); MSBuildTreatWarningsAsErrors promotes warnings raised by MSBuild/SDK
         tasks and pack targets (MSB*, NETSDK*, ...). With only the first, a task-emitted warning would
         still merge unnoticed. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <MSBuildTreatWarningsAsErrors>true</MSBuildTreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- Keep NuGet security-audit advisories (NU1901-NU1905) as warnings: a CVE published overnight
         against a dependency would otherwise turn every PR red without any code change (NuGet honours
         TreatWarningsAsErrors for its own restore warnings, so they ARE promoted by default). The
         advisory still shows in logs; Dependabot, dependency-review and CodeQL are the channels that act
         on it. This exclusion MUST live in WarningsNotAsErrors, not MSBuildWarningsNotAsErrors: NuGet
         reads the compiler-scoped list — the MSBuild-scoped one does not suppress NU* audit errors. -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;NU1905</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Runs the Roslyn code-style analyzers (IDE*) as part of the build. Without this they exist only in the
    IDE: the rules configured in .editorconfig are read, and then nothing reports them at build time. It is
    the switch, .editorconfig is only the dial.

    Deliberately NOT scoped to CI, unlike the warning ratchet above. The point is to make the rule visible
    to whoever writes the code — a human in an editor without ReSharper, or an agent that cannot read a
    .DotSettings file at all — at the moment they build, not once the pull request is already open. In CI
    the same diagnostics are promoted to errors by TreatWarningsAsErrors, so the rule is advisory locally
    and blocking on the way in.

    Only the rules .editorconfig gives an explicit severity are enforced; everything else keeps its default
    (silent or suggestion) and cannot break a build.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    A netstandard2.0 project defaults to C# 7.3, because the SDK derives the language version from
    the target framework. Every source file in this repository would then be rejected for using
    file-scoped namespaces, and the shipped libraries target netstandard2.0 by design (ADR-0001).
    Set here rather than per project so a new catalog cannot forget it and fail for a reason that
    has nothing to do with what it changed.

    Nullable is repo-wide for the same reason: the alternative is each project opting in, and the
    one that forgets is the one that ships a null-reference bug.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Central Package Management is configured in Directory.Packages.props (ManagePackageVersionsCentrally plus
    the <PackageVersion> list). The property is repeated here for a TOOLING reason, not an MSBuild one:
    OpenSSF Scorecard's Pinned-Dependencies check inspects every Directory.*.props file and keeps only the
    LAST one it reads when deciding whether CPM is enabled (it overwrites its config per file). This file is
    read after Directory.Packages.props and carries no <PackageVersion>, so without the property here Scorecard
    concludes "CPM disabled" and reports the solution's `dotnet restore` as an unpinned nugetCommand — capping
    the check at 7. Declaring it here makes the winning file report CPM enabled; its empty version set trivially
    satisfies Scorecard's "all versions are fixed" rule, so the restore counts as pinned. Redundant for MSBuild
    (same value as Directory.Packages.props).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The Roslyn LOAD CONTRACT for the shipped analyzer, and the reason it is a property rather than a
    number in a csproj.

    DiagnosticCatalog.Analyzers is loaded by each consumer's host compiler, not by their application.
    The Microsoft.CodeAnalysis version it is COMPILED against is therefore the minimum Roslyn able to
    load it: build against a higher one and it fails to load with CS8032 on every older SDK and IDE —
    which reports no diagnostics at all, indistinguishable from code that has none.

    4.8.0 is Visual Studio 2022 17.8 / the .NET 8 SDK. It is deliberately far below the 4.14.0 pinned
    centrally for eng/CatalogGen, which is a build-time tool of this repository and constrains nobody
    else; the analyzer takes this floor through a VersionOverride so the two never share a number.

    Declared once here so the csproj pin and the test that guards it (RoslynFloorTests, which reads it
    back through assembly metadata) cannot drift apart.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RoslynFloorVersion>4.8.0</RoslynFloorVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The Sonar C# rules, brought forward from the report to the build.

    Until now they existed only inside the scanner-hooked compilation in .github/workflows/sonar.yml,
    which is the one build in this repository where the warning ratchet above is deliberately turned
    off — so a Sonar rule was met by a contributor, human or agent, after the merge, in a dashboard,
    and never while writing the code. That is the same failure .editorconfig records about a rule kept
    only in a .DotSettings file, and it is how 46 issues accumulated unseen.

    The analyzer is a build-time asset only: PrivateAssets="all" keeps it out of every published
    package's dependency graph.

    build/sonar-profile.globalconfig is GENERATED by tools/sonar-profile/sync-profile.sh from this
    project's SonarQube Cloud quality profile, and lists every rule that profile activates. The
    package alone is NOT the profile — measured here, its default set leaves S3776 disabled although
    the profile activates it, so the four methods reported over the complexity threshold went
    unreported locally until the rule was given an explicit severity. Reading the profile is the only
    way the build and the report can be talking about the same rules.

    The default is ENFORCE: the generated file puts every rule at `warning`, which the ratchet above
    turns into an error in CI. `suggestion` was rejected as the default — at that severity a Sonar
    diagnostic prints nothing in `dotnet build` at any verbosity, so the list would have been
    invisible to the reader it exists for.

    The EXCEPTIONS are in .editorconfig, which wins over a global AnalyzerConfig: `suggestion` for a
    rule whose existing violations are not cleared yet (with its count), `none` for one this codebase
    refuses (with its reason). All 377 rules are enforced as of this commit — the list is empty, and
    that is the state to keep it in. Membership is generated; every exception is written down.
    Decision: ADR-0021.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="SonarAnalyzer.CSharp" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <GlobalAnalyzerConfigFiles Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build/sonar-profile.globalconfig" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
